                <body><div><div class="section" id="ofr20261029-abstract" outline="toc"><a class="toc_anchor usa-link" id="ofr20261029-toc_anchor_1" style="position: relative;"></a><h2 class="publication-title">Abstract</h2><p class="grid">We present a simple method to estimate the risk of experiencing strong shaking from
                  aftershocks during entry into earthquake-damaged buildings. We compute wait times
                  until the probability of strong ground shaking from aftershocks reaches a predefined
                  risk threshold; for example, a 0.4 percent probability of experiencing Modified Mercalli
                  Intensity 7 or greater shaking during the planned building entry. We also develop
                  a relation between aftershock probability and the probability of strong shaking, so
                  that users can reference the U.S. Geological Survey aftershock forecast during an
                  ongoing aftershock sequence to determine if the risk threshold has been met. We apply
                  our method to active continental regions (for example, the Western United States),
                  stable continental regions (for example, the Central and Eastern United States), and
                  subduction zones (for example, Cascadia or Alaska).</p></div><div class="book-part" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1"><div class="body"><div class="section"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b1"><!-- named anchor --></a><h2 class="publication-title" outline="toc"><a class="toc_anchor usa-link" id="ofr20261029-toc_anchor_2" style="position: relative;"></a>Introduction</h2>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b1b3">After a major earthquake, entry into damaged buildings may be necessary for response,
                  recovery, and rebuilding activities. However, aftershocks may further damage or collapse
                  these already-damaged structures, posing a risk to the people entering these buildings.
                  Aftershock forecasts can support decision making around if and when people can enter
                  damaged buildings or the surrounding areas (<a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r3">Becker and others, 2015</a>). Ideally, decision makers would have access to sequence-specific aftershock hazard
                  models that could be used to quantitatively determine the likelihood of strong ground
                  shaking at a given location (for example, <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r9">Gerstenberger and others, 2024</a>). Unfortunately, this type of information is rarely available in real time.</p>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b1b5">Therefore, simple methods are needed to estimate the risk of strong ground shaking
                  from aftershocks, so that users need to access only publicly available information
                  such as the magnitude of the mainshock. <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r8">Gallagher and others (1999)</a> presented a table of wait times for entry into damaged buildings as a function of
                  mainshock magnitude. The wait time is defined as the time after the mainshock when
                  the probability of strong ground shaking from aftershocks has decreased to a specified
                  risk threshold. However, the method used to compute the table in <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r8">Gallagher and others (1999)</a> is not described in their report. Since that publication, there have been significant
                  advances in modeling both aftershock probabilities and earthquake ground motions,
                  as well as their regional variations (for example, <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r19">Page and others, 2016</a>; <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r10">Goulet and others, 2021</a>; <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r5">Bozorgnia and others, 2022</a>). Additionally, the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) now issues public forecasts of
                  the probability and expected number of aftershocks following all magnitude [<i>M</i>]≥5 mainshocks in the United States and its territories (excluding earthquakes related
                  to volcanic activity, such as those in Hawaiʻi), as well as for some destructive international
                  earthquakes.</p>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b1b7">Here we present a method for estimating the risk of strong shaking due to an aftershock,
                  improving upon the single table given by <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r8">Gallagher and others (1999)</a>. We expand the analysis to multiple tectonic regimes: active continental zones (such
                  as the Western United States), stable continental zones (such as the Central and Eastern
                  United States), and subduction zones (such as Cascadia or Alaska). We also develop
                  a relation between aftershock probability and the probability of strong shaking, so
                  that users can reference the USGS aftershock forecast to obtain risk estimates for
                  a specific ongoing aftershock sequence. In our examples, we will use the risk threshold
                  chosen by <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r8">Gallagher and others (1999)</a>, specifically a 0.4 percent probability of experiencing Modified Mercalli Intensity
                  (MMI) 7 or greater shaking during the timeframe of planned building entry. Note that
                  the intended audience of <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r8">Gallagher and others (1999)</a> was mainly engineers involved in post-earthquake building inspection; other groups
                  such as search and rescue may have much higher risk tolerance. Our methods are easily
                  applicable to different risk thresholds.</p>
</div><div class="section"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3"><!-- named anchor --></a><h2 class="publication-title" outline="toc"><a class="toc_anchor usa-link" id="ofr20261029-toc_anchor_3" style="position: relative;"></a>Methods</h2>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b3">To estimate the risk of strong shaking from aftershocks, we compute the probability
                  that the location of a damaged building will experience MMI 7 or greater shaking from
                  aftershocks as a function of the mainshock magnitude <i>M</i><sub>main</sub>, entry duration <i>t</i><sub>e</sub>, and candidate wait time <i>t</i><sub>0</sub>. We then search across candidate wait times to find the time at which the probability
                  matches the chosen risk threshold. Additionally, we compute the probability of MMI
                  7 or greater shaking as a function of mainshock magnitude and the probability of aftershocks.
                  Users can reference the USGS aftershock forecast for a specific ongoing sequence to
                  see if the threshold aftershock probability has been reached.</p>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b5">We start by computing the expected number of occurrences of MMI 7 or greater shaking
                  from aftershocks at a location. To do this, we compute the expected number of aftershocks
                  of magnitude <i>M</i><sub>aft</sub> multiplied by the probability that any single building location will be within the
                  MMI 7 or greater shaking area of an earthquake of magnitude <i>M</i><sub>aft</sub> and sum over the range of aftershock magnitudes. We do not attempt to model the exact
                  locations of the damaged buildings or their spatial relation to the aftershocks. If
                  we assume only that a damaged building is within the MMI 7 footprint of the mainshock,
                  the probability of that building also being within the MMI 7 footprint of an aftershock
                  is given by the ratio of the MMI 7 area of the aftershock to that of the mainshock.
                  Therefore, the expected number of occurrences of MMI 7 or greater shaking from aftershocks
                  is written as:
                  </p><div class="disp-formula panel"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-e01"><!-- named anchor --></a><div class="formula-content"><math id="ofr20261029-m1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
