</mfenced>
</mrow></math><div class="formula-string">,</div></div><h5 class="label">(4)</h5></div>where
                  <div class="def-list"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13"><!-- named anchor --></a><div class="def-list table"><div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;margin-bottom: 2em;"><dl><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13aa"><p><i>N</i><sub>aft</sub></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13ab2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13ab2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the expected number of aftershocks,</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b1a"><p><i>M</i><sub>aft</sub></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b1b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b1b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the aftershock magnitude,</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b2a"><p><i>P</i><sub>forecast</sub></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b2b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b2b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the probability of a <span class="inline-formula"><math id="ofr20261029-m8" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
<mrow>
<mi>M</mi>
<mo>≥</mo>
<msub>
<mi>M</mi>
<mrow>
<mi>f</mi>
<mi>o</mi>
<mi>r</mi>
<mi>e</mi>
<mi>c</mi>
<mi>a</mi>
<mi>s</mi>
<mi>t</mi>
</mrow>
</msub>
</mrow></math></span> aftershock within the next day,</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b3a"><p><i>b</i></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b3b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b3b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the seismicity b-value,</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b4a"><p><i>M</i><sub>forecast</sub></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b4b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b4b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the forecast aftershock magnitude, and</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b5a"><p><span class="inline-formula"><math id="ofr20261029-m9" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
<mrow>
<mi>Δ</mi>
<mi>M</mi>
</mrow></math></span></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b5b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c13b5b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the magnitude discretization.</p></dd></dl></div></div>
</div>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3b9c15">The probability of experiencing MMI 7 or greater shaking can then be computed as in
                     <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-e01">equations 1</a> and <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-e02">2</a>, with the dependencies on times <span class="inline-formula"><math id="ofr20261029-m10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
<mrow>
<msub>
<mi>t</mi>
<mn>0</mn>
</msub>
</mrow></math></span> and <span class="inline-formula"><math id="ofr20261029-m11" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
<mrow>
<msub>
<mi>t</mi>
<mtext>e</mtext>
</msub>
</mrow></math></span> replaced by a dependency on the aftershock probability <i>P</i><sub>forecast</sub>. We then search across candidate values of <i>P</i><sub>forecast</sub> to find the aftershock probability that matches the chosen risk threshold.</p>
</div>
<div class="section"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11"><!-- named anchor --></a><h3 class="series-title">Area Experiencing Strong Shaking</h3>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b3">The area of MMI 7 or greater shaking as a function of magnitude is determined using
                     existing Ground Motion Models (GMMs) and simple assumptions about the earthquake rupture
                     geometry and site conditions. For active continental regions, we sample ground motions
                     from the Next Generation Attenuation (NGA)-West2 models of <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r4">Boore and others (2014)</a>, <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r6">Campbell and Bozorgnia (2014)</a>, <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r1">Abrahamson and others (2014)</a>, and <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r7">Chiou and Youngs (2014)</a>. For stable continental regions, we sample the NGA-East model (<a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r10">Goulet and others, 2021</a>), which combines 17 GMMs (for example, <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r27">Yenier and Atkinson, 2015</a>; <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r12">Hassani and Atkinson, 2015</a>). We use this model together with the linear and nonlinear site amplification models
                     developed by <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r23">Stewart and others (2020)</a> and <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r11">Hashash and others (2020)</a>, respectively. For subduction zones, we sample the NGA-Subduction models of <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r20">Parker and others (2022)</a> and <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r14">Kuehn and others (2023)</a>. These models return Peak Ground Velocity (PGV), rather than MMI, so we use the magnitude
                     and distance dependent relations of <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r26">Worden and others (2012)</a> to convert PGV to MMI. We use the model by <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r2">Atkinson and Kaka (2007)</a> to convert PGV to MMI for stable continental regions. We then find the source-to-site
                     distance corresponding to MMI 7 shaking as a function of magnitude.</p>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b5">The area experiencing MMI 7 or greater shaking from a magnitude <i>M</i> earthquake corresponds to the region where the source-to-site distance is less than
                     the computed threshold. For crustal earthquakes in both active and stable regions,
                     we assume a vertical planar rupture. Consequently, the area is given by:
                     </p><div class="disp-formula panel"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-e05"><!-- named anchor --></a><div class="formula-content"><math id="ofr20261029-m12" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
<mrow>
<msub>
<mi>A</mi>
<mrow>
<mi>M</mi>
<mi>M</mi>
<mi>I</mi>
<mn>7</mn>
</mrow>
</msub>
<mfenced>
<mi>M</mi>
</mfenced>
<mo>=</mo>
