</mrow></math><div class="formula-string">,</div></div><h5 class="label">(5)</h5></div>where
                  <div class="def-list"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7"><!-- named anchor --></a><div class="def-list table"><div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;margin-bottom: 2em;"><dl><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7aa"><p><i>A</i><sub>MMI7</sub></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7ab2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7ab2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the area of expected MMI 7 or greater ground motion,</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b1a"><p><i>M</i></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b1b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b1b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the earthquake magnitude,</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b2a"><p><i>R</i><sub>JB</sub></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b2b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b2b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the Joyner-Boore distance to MMI 7 for magnitude <i>M</i>, and</p></dd><dt class="def-term cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b3a"><p><i>L</i></p></dt><dd class="def-def cell" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b3b2"><p class="" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b7b3b2b1" style="margin-block-end: -1em;">is the fault length for magnitude <i>M</i> from <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r25">Wells and Coppersmith (1994)</a>.</p></dd></dl></div></div>
</div>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11b9">For subduction zone earthquakes, we assume a dipping planar rupture, with the geometry
                     defined by the dip and the depth of the top of the rupture, as well as the horizontal
                     location relative to the coastline. The length and width of the rupture are a function
                     of magnitude and are taken from <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r24">Strasser and others (2010)</a>. Because the subduction GMMs use rupture distance instead of Joyner-Boore distance,
                     we compute the area experiencing MMI 7 or greater shaking as the region on the land
                     surface that has a minimum distance to the rupture plane shorter than the computed
                     distance threshold. The dipping subduction zone geometry does not allow a simple solution
                     like in <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-e05">equation 5</a>.</p>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c11c11">In our subduction examples, we use a geometry representative of the Cascadia subduction
                     zone and the Alaska subduction zone near Anchorage. The subduction interface is modeled
                     to be at 20±5 kilometers (km) depth beneath the coastline; in Alaska, it is defined
                     as the outer coastlines of the Kenai Peninsula and Kodiak Island (<a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r16">McCrory and others, 2012</a>; <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r13">Hayes and others, 2018</a>). The interface dip and its uncertainty are 12±8.5° (<a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r13">Hayes and others, 2018</a>). Subduction interface mainshocks are modeled to occur anywhere along this fault
                     as long as the finite rupture is fully contained between the surface and 55 km depth
                     (<a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r15">Lay and others, 2012</a>). Subduction intraslab mainshocks are modeled to occur at depths from 40 to 140 km,
                     with a dip uniformly distributed between 5° and 85°. Aftershock depths are limited
                     by assuming the aftershock rate decays with distance from the mainshock as described
                     in <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r28">Zhang and others (2018)</a>. For interface mainshocks, 15±10 percent of aftershocks are assumed to occur in the
                     upper plate and are modeled as active continental earthquakes.</p>
</div>
<div class="section"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c13"><!-- named anchor --></a><h3 class="series-title">Uncertainty</h3>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b3c13b3">The models for the expected number of aftershocks and the area of MMI 7 or greater
                     shaking both include uncertainty. We propagate this into uncertainty in the wait time
                     <i>t<sub>0</sub></i> and threshold aftershock probability <i>P</i><sub>forecast</sub> by calculating 10,000 Monte Carlo realizations. For each realization, we resample
                     the aftershock productivity <i>a</i>-value, the seismicity <i>b</i>-value, the choice of GMM, the event term from the GMM, the choice of <i>V</i><sub>s30</sub> (average shear wave velocity in the upper 30 m, sampled uniformly between 150 meters
                     per second [m/s] and 1,000 m/s), and the fault geometry and rate of upper plate aftershocks
                     for subduction zone events. For each candidate wait time <i>t</i><sub>0</sub> or candidate aftershock probability <i>P</i><sub>forecast</sub>, we find the median and the 84th percentile of the probability of strong shaking
                     <i>P</i><sub>MMI7</sub> over all realizations with <i>A</i><sub>MMI7</sub>(<i>M</i><sub>main</sub>)&gt;0. We report the values of <i>t</i><sub>0</sub> and <i>P</i><sub>forecast</sub> for which the median and 84th percentile probability of strong shaking match the
                     chosen risk threshold of 0.4 percent.</p>
</div>
</div><div class="section"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b5"><!-- named anchor --></a><h2 class="publication-title" outline="toc"><a class="toc_anchor usa-link" id="ofr20261029-toc_anchor_4" style="position: relative;"></a>Calculated Wait Times and Threshold Aftershock Probabilities</h2>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b5b3">The median and 84th percentile wait times until the probability of MMI 7 or greater
                  shaking at a single location reaches the threshold of 0.4 percent are shown in <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-t01">table 1</a> for continental mainshocks. The wait times increase for larger magnitude mainshocks,
                  reflecting the increase in aftershock rate with mainshock magnitude which outpaces
                  the increase in the MMI 7 footprint of the mainshock. The wait times for active continental
                  regions tend to be longer than the wait times for stable continental regions, reflecting
                  higher aftershock productivity. The NGA-East models have substantially larger MMI
                  7 areas than the NGA-West2 models, but the area ratios between mainshocks and aftershocks
                  are similar, so this has only a small effect on the results.</p>
<div class="table-wrap panel" style="display: float; clear: both"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-t01"><!-- named anchor --></a>
<div><h3 style="display:inline;font-size: 1em;">Table 1.    </h3>Wait time (rounded to the nearest day) until probability of Modified Mercalli Intensity
                        (MMI) 7 or greater shaking from aftershocks is at or below 0.4 percent during the
                        entry period, for crustal mainshocks.</div><sup class="caption" style="font-size: 85%;top: 0.5em;">
<h4 aria-hidden="true" class="subsection-title"></h4>

