<caption style="display: none;">Table 4.    Threshold probability of a magnitude [M]≥6 aftershock within the next day, for subduction zone mainshocks. Waiting until
                        the forecast probability is at or below this threshold achieves a 0.4 percent or lower
                        probability of Modified Mercalli Intensity (MMI) 7 or greater shaking from aftershocks
                        during the next day.</caption></table></div>
</div>
</div><div class="section"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b7"><!-- named anchor --></a><h2 class="publication-title" outline="toc"><a class="toc_anchor usa-link" id="ofr20261029-toc_anchor_5" style="position: relative;"></a>Discussion</h2>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b7b3">Our method to calculate the risk of strong ground shaking from aftershocks is not
                  specific to a particular aftershock sequence and contains a number of generalizations
                  and simplifications. If an aftershock hazard model specific to an ongoing sequence
                  were available, that information would likely be more accurate. The main simplifications
                  in our method are the assumptions about the mainshock and aftershock geometry. Crustal
                  earthquakes are modeled as vertical planar faults, whereas subduction zone earthquakes
                  are modeled as dipping planar faults. We do not model any spatial relation between
                  aftershocks and damaged buildings, other than that both are located inside the MMI
                  7 shaking footprint of the mainshock. Aftershock rates are known to decay with distance
                  from the mainshock (for example, <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r18">Ogata and Zhuang, 2006</a>), however no similar models are available for the locations of damaged buildings.
                  As a further simplification, we use the aftershock rate model by <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r22">Reasenberg and Jones (1989)</a>, which neglects the secondary triggering of aftershocks by prior aftershocks. Some
                  site parameters, such as basin depth, were assigned default values to represent average
                  conditions. In site-specific applications, incorporating actual data would better
                  capture site-to-site variability.</p>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b7b5">The tables presented in this report apply to active continental, stable continental,
                  and subduction zone settings, which cover most but not all of the seismically active
                  regions of the United States and the world. In the United States, the aftershock risk
                  in Hawaiʻi requires special consideration because many earthquake sequences there
                  are related to volcanic processes and are not well-approximated by the model by <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r22">Reasenberg and Jones (1989)</a>. <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r21">Petersen and others (2022)</a> determined that the NGA-West2 GMMs are appropriate for shallow (depth ≤ 20 km) earthquakes
                  in Hawaiʻi, implying that the Active Continental probability thresholds in <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-t03">table 3</a> are applicable for non-volcanic shallow earthquakes. The Active Continental wait
                  times in <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-t01">table 1</a> could be used as conservative estimates, because the aftershock productivity for
                  hotspots like Hawaiʻi is lower than for active continental regions (<a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r19">Page and others, 2016</a>). Puerto Rico and other islands in subduction settings are not exactly analogous
                  to the Cascadia/Alaska model used here, as their land areas sample less of the potential
                  shaking area. The aftershock risk for each island would vary with its proximity to
                  the subduction interface. Location-specific tables could be developed following our
                  methodology for any location where the geometry can be modeled and <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r22">Reasenberg and Jones (1989)</a> parameter values and GMMs are available.</p>
</div><div class="section"><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b9"><!-- named anchor --></a><h2 class="publication-title" outline="toc"><a class="toc_anchor usa-link" id="ofr20261029-toc_anchor_6" style="position: relative;"></a>Summary</h2>
<p id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b7b1b1b9b3">We have introduced a method to estimate the risk of strong ground shaking from aftershocks
                  during entry into earthquake-damaged buildings. We have applied this method to three
                  tectonic regimes: active continental (Western United States), stable continental (Central
                  and Eastern United States), and subduction zone (Cascadia or Alaska). The results
                  are posed as a wait time until the risk reaches a given threshold, or alternatively
                  as the aftershock probability corresponding to the given risk threshold. In our examples,
                  we use the risk threshold of a 0.4 percent probability of experiencing Modified Mercalli
                  Intensity (MMI) 7 or greater shaking, as proposed by <a class="usa-link" href="#ofr20261029-r8">Gallagher and others (1999)</a>. Our method is easily applicable to other risk thresholds. The resulting tables can
                  be used to assess risk using only publicly available information, namely the magnitude
                  of the mainshock or the U.S. Geological Survey aftershock forecast. Because our method
                  is general and relies on some simplifications, a sequence-specific aftershock hazard
                  model for an ongoing sequence, if available, would likely contain more accurate information
                  about aftershock risk during that sequence.</p>
</div></div></div><div class="back-section" outline="toc"><a class="toc_anchor usa-link" id="ofr20261029-toc_anchor_7" style="position: relative;"></a><a class="usa-link" id="ofr20261029-w56ab1b9b1"><!-- named anchor --></a><h2 class="publication-title">Acknowledgments</h2>
